{% extends "guide-caution.njk" %}

{% block section_0 %}
<p>
  South Korea has one of the world's most advanced digital economies — with near-universal smartphone penetration, extremely fast internet infrastructure, and a highly engaged online population of 50 million people.<br>
  But Korea's internet ecosystem operates on fundamentally different rails from the Western web. The dominant search engine is Naver, not Google. The dominant messaging and payment platform is Kakao, not WhatsApp or Apple Pay. And Korean consumers have specific design expectations and trust signals that differ significantly from Western norms.<br>
  This guide covers what you need to build a Korean website that gets found, earns trust, and converts — rather than simply displaying Korean text on a Western-format page.
</p>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_1_1 %}
<p>
  Korean is a single standardized language with relatively little regional variation in writing — but register (formal vs. informal) is critically important and culturally loaded.
</p>
<ul style="list-style: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-bottom: 24px;">
  <li><strong>Formal polite speech (합쇼체 / habnida-che):</strong> The standard for B2B, official communications, and any website targeting professional or older audiences. Uses ~합니다 / ~입니다 sentence endings. This is generally the safe default for business websites.</li>
  <li><strong>Informal polite speech (해요체 / haeyo-che):</strong> Warmer and more approachable. Common in B2C, lifestyle brands, and platforms targeting younger audiences. Uses ~해요 / ~예요 endings.</li>
  <li><strong>Avoid casual or informal speech (반말) entirely:</strong> Using 반말 on a business website — even to seem friendly — is perceived as disrespectful to most Korean adults.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  Set <code>&lt;html lang="ko"&gt;</code> on your page. Korean text rendering is sensitive to font selection — machine translation from English often produces stilted or unnatural Korean. Native review is essential, particularly for marketing copy and CTAs.
</p>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_1_2 %}
<p>
  Korea has one of the world's fastest internet networks, so raw load speed is less of a concern than in many markets. However, there are platform-specific compatibility issues to be aware of:
</p>
<div style="background:#fff4f4; padding:16px; border-radius:8px; border:1px solid #f9a8a8; margin-bottom:24px;">
  <p style="font-weight:bold; color:#c53030; margin-bottom:8px;">❌ Common technical issues in Korea</p>
  <ul style="list-style:none; padding:0; margin:0;">
    <li style="margin-bottom:8px;">· KakaoTalk's in-app browser (카카오톡 인앱 브라우저) renders some CSS differently than Chrome or Safari — test your site there specifically</li>
    <li style="margin-bottom:8px;">· Naver Blog and Naver Cafe previews use Open Graph tags — ensure og:title, og:description, and og:image are correctly set</li>
    <li>· Some older Korean enterprise environments still use Internet Explorer — test for IE compatibility if targeting corporate B2B</li>
  </ul>
</div>
<p>
  Korea has largely moved away from the Active-X dependency that plagued Korean websites for decades, but a small number of financial and government sites still require specific browser configurations. For general commercial websites, modern web standards apply.
</p>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_1_3 %}
<p>
  South Korea has robust personal data protection legislation:
</p>
<ul style="list-style: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-bottom: 24px;">
  <li><strong>Personal Information Protection Act (개인정보 보호법, PIPA):</strong> Korea's primary data protection law, significantly strengthened in 2023. Requires explicit consent before collecting personal data, a clearly stated privacy policy in Korean, and specific obligations around data retention and breach notification. The Korean Personal Information Protection Commission (PIPC) actively enforces compliance.</li>
  <li><strong>Act on Promotion of Information and Communications Network Utilization (정보통신망법):</strong> Governs online service providers. Requires displaying business registration information, including the operator's name, address, and business registration number (사업자등록번호) on the website.</li>
  <li><strong>E-commerce regulations:</strong> Korean consumer protection law (전자상거래법) mandates clear return policies, cooling-off rights, and specific order confirmation processes for online purchases.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  If you're collecting any personal information from Korean users — even just an email newsletter signup — Korean language compliance disclosures are legally required.
</p>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_2_1 %}
<p>
  Korean digital consumers are sophisticated and demanding. They conduct thorough research before making purchasing decisions, and trust signals that work in Western markets often don't translate directly.
</p>
<ul style="list-style: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
  <li><strong>Business registration number (사업자등록번호):</strong> Displaying your Korean business registration number in the footer is a legal requirement for businesses operating in Korea — and a crucial trust signal. Users actively check these. If you don't have one, partnering with a Korean distributor or representative who does can provide this credibility layer.</li>
  <li><strong>Detailed product/service information:</strong> Korean buyers expect exhaustive detail. Specification tables, comparison charts, usage scenarios, and FAQ sections are expected, not optional.</li>
  <li><strong>Naver-specific content:</strong> Naver Blog posts and Naver Knowledge (지식인) answers that reference your brand or product appear prominently in Naver search results. Creating or sponsoring Naver content is a core part of Korean content marketing.</li>
  <li><strong>Review culture:</strong> Korean consumers heavily rely on reviews. Kakao Maps reviews, Naver Place reviews, and dedicated review platforms like Naver Shopping are major purchase influencers.</li>
</ul>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_2_2 %}
<p>
  <strong>Naver controls approximately 55–65% of Korean search market share</strong> — significantly more than Google in Korea (which holds roughly 30%). SEO for Korea therefore means Naver SEO first:
</p>
<ul style="list-style: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
  <li><strong>Naver Search Advisor (네이버 서치어드바이저):</strong> The Korean equivalent of Google Search Console. Submit your sitemap and verify your site here. Essential for any website targeting Korean users.</li>
  <li><strong>Naver Blog integration:</strong> Naver's own blog platform (네이버 블로그) ranks extremely well in Naver search. A Naver Blog associated with your brand is often more discoverable than your own website for Korean search queries.</li>
  <li><strong>Naver Shopping (네이버 쇼핑):</strong> For e-commerce, product listings on Naver Shopping outperform standalone website organic rankings significantly. Integration is essential for Korean e-commerce.</li>
  <li><strong>Naver keyword research:</strong> Use Naver's keyword tool (네이버 키워드 도구) for Korean search volume data — Google Keyword Planner does not reflect Naver search patterns accurately.</li>
  <li><strong>Google SEO still matters:</strong> Especially for younger demographics (20s–30s) and B2B audiences who may prefer Google. Don't ignore Google SEO entirely.</li>
</ul>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_2_3 %}
<p>
  Korea has excellent internet infrastructure, and latency from nearby East Asian data centers is generally acceptable. For serious Korean market investment, however:
</p>
<p>
  <strong>Korean data center hosting</strong> offers the fastest load times and the strongest SEO signal for Naver (which, like Baidu, shows preference for locally hosted content). Major Korean hosting providers include Cafe24, Gabia, and Dothome. AWS Seoul (ap-northeast-2) and Google Cloud Seoul are also viable options with Korean data residency.
</p>
<p>
  For businesses without Korean hosting, a CDN with Korean PoPs (Cloudflare has a Seoul presence) can significantly reduce latency. Domain choice: <code>.co.kr</code> is the standard Korean domain and conveys local presence — it requires a Korean business registration. <code>.com</code> is also widely accepted and trusted by Korean users.
</p>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_3_1 %}
<p>
  Korean design aesthetics blend Eastern and Western influences in a distinctive way — clean and modern, but with specific color and visual conventions:
</p>
<ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0;">
  <li style="margin-bottom: 12px;">🔵 <strong>Blue:</strong> The most trusted business color — used heavily in Korean corporate, financial, and technology sectors. Associated with reliability and professionalism.</li>
  <li style="margin-bottom: 12px;">🔴 <strong>Red:</strong> Bold and energetic. Common for promotions, sale badges, and urgency signals. Also carries positive associations as part of Korean national identity (태극기).</li>
  <li style="margin-bottom: 12px;">⚪ <strong>White and clean backgrounds:</strong> Minimalism is appreciated in Korean design, particularly in tech and lifestyle brands. White space signals quality and modernity.</li>
  <li style="margin-bottom: 12px;">🟡 <strong>Yellow / Kakao yellow (#FAE100):</strong> Strongly associated with Kakao (Korea's dominant platform). If you're not integrating KakaoTalk, avoid this specific yellow tone to prevent brand confusion.</li>
</ul>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_3_2 %}
<p>
  Korean requires Hangul-optimized fonts — Latin fonts do not apply to the Korean script. The recommended font stack:
</p>
<div style="background:#f1f5f9; padding:12px; border-radius:4px; font-family:monospace; margin-bottom:16px;">
  font-family: "Noto Sans KR", "Apple SD Gothic Neo", "Malgun Gothic", sans-serif;
</div>
<p>
  "Noto Sans KR" is Google's Korean font and covers all Hangul characters with clean, modern rendering. It's available via Google Fonts and is the most widely used web font for Korean content. "Apple SD Gothic Neo" is Apple's native Korean font (macOS/iOS), and "Malgun Gothic" is the Windows standard.
</p>
<p>
  Korean body text works best at <strong>14–16px</strong> with a line-height of <strong>1.7–1.9</strong>. Hangul characters are more visually complex than Latin letters, so generous line spacing improves readability significantly. Letter-spacing of 0–0.05em is standard — avoid negative letter-spacing for Korean text.
</p>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_3_3 %}
<p>
  Korean web design has evolved toward a distinctive style that balances information density with visual polish:
</p>
<ul style="list-style: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
  <li><strong>Detailed content is expected:</strong> Korean users conduct thorough research before purchasing. Unlike Western minimal landing pages, Korean product and service pages typically include extensive tabbed content, detailed specifications, and multiple comparison views.</li>
  <li><strong>Mobile card-based layout:</strong> On mobile, Korean apps and sites favor a card-based scrollable layout (influenced by KakaoTalk and Naver's UI patterns). Clean card components with clear typography are the standard.</li>
  <li><strong>Trust badge placement:</strong> Certification marks, awards, and partner logos are displayed prominently — often more visibly than on Western equivalents. These visually anchor credibility for Korean audiences.</li>
  <li><strong>Promotion and discount visibility:</strong> Korean e-commerce norms prominently feature pricing, discount percentages, and coupon availability. Hiding or de-emphasizing pricing is unusual and can reduce conversion.</li>
</ul>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_4_1 %}
<p>
  Korea's communication ecosystem centers on <strong>KakaoTalk (카카오톡)</strong> — the dominant messaging app used by virtually all Korean smartphone users. Effective Korean websites integrate KakaoTalk as a primary contact channel:
</p>
<ul style="list-style: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
  <li><strong>KakaoTalk Channel (카카오톡 채널):</strong> A business profile on KakaoTalk that users can add as a "friend." The Korean equivalent of a WeChat Official Account. Displays a distinctive yellow button on your website. Essential for any B2C business in Korea.</li>
  <li><strong>KakaoTalk Chat button:</strong> A floating chat button using the KakaoTalk Channel API is the highest-converting CTA for Korean mobile users — significantly more effective than a contact form.</li>
  <li><strong>Naver Talk Talk (네이버 톡톡):</strong> Naver's built-in chat feature for businesses listed on Naver. Enables real-time chat directly from Naver search results — important for businesses appearing in Naver Shopping or Local.</li>
  <li><strong>Phone and SMS:</strong> Korean business culture still values direct phone contact. A Korean phone number in the header is a strong trust signal, especially for B2B.</li>
</ul>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_4_2 %}
<p>
  South Korea has <strong>the world's highest smartphone penetration rate</strong> — over 95% of adults use a smartphone, and mobile internet usage far exceeds desktop. Korean users are particularly active on mobile for shopping, information search, and communication.
</p>
<p>
  Critical mobile considerations for Korean websites:
</p>
<ul style="list-style: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
  <li><strong>KakaoTalk in-app browser:</strong> A very large percentage of Korean web traffic arrives through links shared on KakaoTalk. KakaoTalk's built-in browser (인앱 브라우저) has specific rendering quirks — test your site there as a priority.</li>
  <li><strong>Naver app browser:</strong> Similarly, links from Naver search often open in Naver's in-app browser. Verify your site functions and displays correctly in Naver's browser.</li>
  <li><strong>Fast scroll performance:</strong> Korean mobile users scroll quickly and expect immediate response. Heavy animations or slow-loading components cause disproportionately high bounce rates.</li>
  <li><strong>Tap targets:</strong> Minimum 44×44px. KakaoTalk chat buttons should be large and persistently visible — often as a floating action button.</li>
</ul>
{% endblock %}

